{
  "$schema": "https://raw.githubusercontent.com/jetify-com/devbox/0.17.5/.schema/devbox.schema.json",
  "packages": ["nodejs@22"],
  "env": {
    "COREPACK_ENABLE_DOWNLOAD_PROMPT": "0"
  },
  "shell": {
    "init_hook": [
      "corepack enable --install-directory \"$DEVBOX_PROJECT_ROOT/.devbox/virtenv/nodejs/corepack-bin\""
    ],
    "scripts": {
      "install": "pnpm install --frozen-lockfile",
      "db": "node scripts/ensure-env.ts && docker compose up -d --wait db",
      "db:down": "docker compose down -v",
      // Explanations here are real comments, never `"// …"` keys: devbox turns every key
      // into a script and eats the leading slashes, so such a key becomes the command it
      // meant to document. See AGENTS.md § Development and issue #30.

      // Every script below that runs a binary out of `node_modules` opens with
      // `sh scripts/require-install.sh <its own name> &&`. A checkout that has never
      // installed is an ordinary state — a fresh clone, a `git worktree add`, an agent's
      // worktree — and what those scripts did in it was fail with `Command "biome" not
      // found`: a message naming a binary, leaving the reader to work out that a package
      // manager never ran, on the command AGENTS.md tells a reader to reach for first
      // (#133). `install` and `ci` carry no guard, because they *are* the install, and a
      // guard on either would refuse the way out of the state it exists to report.

      // A worktree gets ports of its own, and it gets them by being given a `.env`.
      // `scripts/ensure-env.ts` writes one — from `.env.example`, once, never over an
      // existing file — so `docker compose` reads the derived ports itself and a hand-typed
      // `docker compose up db` reaches the same container these scripts do. Everywhere else
      // it does nothing at all, and the ports are `compose.yaml`'s ordinary `${…:-}`
      // fallbacks. See docs/adr/0084-a-linked-worktree-seeds-its-own-dot-env.md.
      //
      // It is chained onto the commands that bring something up rather than run from
      // `postinstall`, which would fire inside the image build where there may be neither
      // `git` nor `.git`, or from a `pre*` script, which pnpm does not run by default.

      // db:generate builds first because a Plugin's drizzle.config.ts imports
      // defineKobaiDrizzleConfig from @kobai/core/migrations, and that specifier resolves
      // through the package's `exports` to dist — the same path a Plugin author outside
      // this repository takes. Recursive because every package that owns tables generates
      // its own migrations from its own schema, Core included.
      "db:generate": "sh scripts/require-install.sh db:generate && pnpm -r build && pnpm -r db:generate",

      // There is deliberately no db:push script. This is the file kobai's commands live
      // in, so it is the file where someone would reach for the convenient one.
      // `drizzle-kit push` diffs against the LIVE database: run against a database
      // holding Plugin tables it reports success and silently drops them, leaving their
      // tracking rows so the migration runner believes the work is done and cannot repair
      // it. Generate a migration instead. Observed on branch
      // prototype/drizzle-multi-migration, FINDINGS.md section E; see
      // docs/adr/0030-generate-and-migrate-only-never-drizzle-kit-push.md.
      // tests/no-push-script.test.ts fails the build if a push script appears here.

      // openapi:generate needs neither a build nor a database: Node runs the TypeScript
      // directly, and the description is a property of the routes rather than of anything
      // behind them. Recursive, and the order is not incidental — pnpm walks the workspace
      // in dependency order, so @kobai/core writes openapi.json and @kobai/client then
      // regenerates itself from what Core wrote. Both files are checked in, and their
      // tests fail the gate when either has drifted.
      "openapi:generate": "sh scripts/require-install.sh openapi:generate && pnpm -r openapi:generate",

      // Rewrites what `create-kobai` generates from the reference Project, which is the
      // source of truth for both. Run it after changing anything under `reference/` — the
      // gate fails until you do, because
      // tests/create-kobai-matches-the-reference-project.test.ts regenerates the template
      // and compares it byte for byte with what is checked in.
      "template:generate": "sh scripts/require-install.sh template:generate && pnpm --filter create-kobai template:generate",

      // `build` then `up` rather than `up --build`, so the address is printed between the
      // build output and the log stream rather than scrolling away above a cold build. It
      // is printed by a script rather than by the shell because the port may live in `.env`,
      // which compose reads and the shell does not — an address printed from a different
      // source than the one compose publishes on would be worse than no address at all.
      "up": "node scripts/ensure-env.ts && docker compose build && node scripts/serving-on.ts && docker compose up",
      "down": "docker compose down",
      "build": "sh scripts/require-install.sh build && pnpm -r build",

      // `scripts/dev.ts` exists to assemble one variable: DATABASE_URL, from the parts, at
      // the moment it is needed. Nothing exports one any more, because a written-down URL
      // goes stale the moment the password above it changes (#63, ADR-0084).
      "dev": "sh scripts/require-install.sh dev && node scripts/ensure-env.ts && pnpm -r build && node scripts/dev.ts",

      // The Admin, with a reload loop, in a second terminal beside `devbox run dev`.
      //
      // It is a Vite dev server rather than the Project's process — nothing else gives a
      // Developer a reload — and it proxies `/admin`, `/store` and `/health` to the Project
      // on PORT. So the **browser still sees exactly one origin**, this one, which is what
      // production looks like too and why no CORS is configured anywhere (ADR-0010,
      // ADR-0033). A second origin would need CORS and would be a dev-only lie about the
      // deployment.
      //
      // `devbox run dev` already serves the *built* Admin at /admin-ui, because it builds
      // first. This is what to run when the Admin is the thing being edited.
      "admin:dev": "sh scripts/require-install.sh admin:dev && pnpm --filter kobai-reference-admin dev",
      // `--error-on-warnings` because `biome ci` exits *zero* on warnings without it, and
      // Biome 2 re-tiered most of what Biome 1 called an error down to `warn` or `info`.
      // The flag covers the `warn` tier and every rule Biome adds to it later; `biome.json`
      // lifts the `info` tier up to `warn`, which no flag can do. Together they mean the
      // gate fails on everything Biome reports. `lint` and `ci` run the identical
      // invocation on purpose — a gate stricter than the command a Developer is told to run
      // is the same invisible difference in a new place. See
      // docs/adr/0039-the-lint-gate-fails-on-every-finding.md and AGENTS.md § Development;
      // tests/the-lint-gate-fails-below-error.test.ts fails the build if either drifts.
      //
      // `format` is deliberately the forgiving one: it rewrites rather than reports, so it
      // is where a finding gets fixed rather than tolerated.
      // The browser `tests/the-admin-in-a-browser.test.ts` drives. Playwright ships no
      // browser in its npm package, so this is a download rather than an install, and it is
      // cached under the machine's own Playwright directory — running it twice costs a
      // version check.
      //
      // `--only-shell chromium` is Chromium's headless shell and not the whole browser: the
      // seam is headless by definition, the shell is a third of the download, and it starts
      // in a fraction of the time. It is asked for by name at launch — `channel:
      // "chromium-headless-shell"` in `tests/support/admin-browser.ts` — because since
      // Playwright 1.49 a bare `headless: true` launches the *full* browser in its new
      // headless mode, which this deliberately does not download. Add `chromium` here to
      // watch a case run in a window.
      //
      // `ci` and `test` run it themselves rather than leaving it to whoever remembers:
      // ADR-0044's line is that a guardrail behind an opt-in step is not a faster guardrail,
      // it is an optional one.
      "browsers": "sh scripts/require-install.sh browsers && pnpm exec playwright install --only-shell chromium",
      "lint": "sh scripts/require-install.sh lint && pnpm exec biome ci . --error-on-warnings",
      "format": "sh scripts/require-install.sh format && pnpm exec biome check --write .",
      "typecheck": "sh scripts/require-install.sh typecheck && pnpm -r typecheck && pnpm exec tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit",
      "test": "sh scripts/require-install.sh test && node scripts/ensure-env.ts && docker compose up -d --wait db && pnpm -r build && pnpm exec playwright install --only-shell chromium && pnpm exec vitest run",
      "ci": "pnpm install --frozen-lockfile && node scripts/ensure-env.ts && docker compose up -d --wait db && pnpm exec biome ci . --error-on-warnings && pnpm -r typecheck && pnpm exec tsc -p tsconfig.json --noEmit && pnpm -r build && pnpm exec playwright install --only-shell chromium && pnpm exec vitest run"
    }
  }
}
